  <!-- ============ READING THE DIAGRAM ============ -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">Reading the diagram</p>
        <h2>The four views, in words</h2>

        <h3>① Die map — compute is an island</h3>
        <p>
          The A100 die packs 108 SMs, each with 4 third-generation tensor cores, a 256 KB
          register file, and 192 KB of combined L1/shared storage (up to 164 KB configurable
          as shared memory). All of them talk to a 40 MB L2 split into two partitions, and the
          L2 talks to five active HBM2e stacks sitting <em>physically beside</em> the die on a
          silicon interposer. The geography is the point: FLOPs are manufactured in the middle,
          but every byte of an LLM's weights lives off-die and must be imported at 1.555 TB/s —
          the width of that bridge, not the size of the island, decides how fast you decode.
        </p>

        <h3>② Hierarchy ladder — multiplicative trades</h3>
        <p>
          Each level trades roughly three orders of magnitude of capacity for one order of
          magnitude of bandwidth and latency. The numbers worth memorizing: shared memory
          aggregates to ≈19.5 TB/s (about 12× HBM), and the register file, at ≈27 MB die-wide,
          is a <em>faster and nearly as large</em> pool as L2 — which is why high-performance
          GEMM kernels are really register-file choreography. The ladder also explains why
          caching weights is hopeless: the largest on-die pool (L2, 40 MB) barely holds one
          4096×4096 FP16 matrix (33.5 MB) out of a 13.5 GB model.
        </p>

        <h3>③ Roofline — one model, two regimes</h3>
        <p>
          Arithmetic intensity (AI) is FLOPs performed per byte moved from HBM. The machine's
          balance point is 312 TFLOP/s ÷ 1.555 TB/s ≈ 200 FLOP/byte. Prefill GEMMs (thousands
          of tokens sharing each weight read) sit far right, compute-bound. Single-batch decode
          is a GEMV: 2 FLOPs per 2-byte weight, AI ≈ 1, achieving ≈1.6 TFLOP/s — half a percent
          of peak, and no kernel cleverness can fix it because the bytes are mandatory.
          Batching amortizes weight reads (AI ≈ B), which is why serving systems fight for
          large batches; attention over a long KV cache, however, stays at AI ≈ 1 even when
          batched, because each sequence reads its <em>own</em> KV — this is exactly the seam
          that KV-cache compression and quantization attack.
        </p>

        <h3>④ One decode step — the mandatory 15 GB</h3>
        <p>
          For a 7B-class FP16 model with 4k context at batch 1, one token costs ≈13.5 GB of
          weights + ≈2.15 GB of KV cache + a 262 MB LM head read — ≈15.4 GB of traffic for
          ≈15 GFLOP of work. Divide: 15.4 GB ÷ 1.555 TB/s ≈ 9.9 ms, an upper bound near
          ~100 tok/s, while the same FLOPs would take the tensor cores ~0.05 ms. Everything in
          efficient inference follows from this division — quantize weights and KV (shrink the
          numerator), batch requests (amortize it), or restructure attention so fewer bytes are
          mandatory. My research lives in that numerator.
        </p>

        <h3>Fine print on the numbers</h3>
        <p>
          Peak specs (108 SMs, 40 GB @ 1.555 TB/s, 40 MB L2, 312 TFLOP/s dense BF16/FP16 tensor
          core, 192 KB L1+shared per SM with ≤164 KB shared) are from NVIDIA's A100 white paper
          for the SXM 40 GB part. On-die bandwidth and latency figures (L2 and shared-memory
          throughput, ~20/200/450 ns tiers) are microbenchmark-derived ballparks that vary with
          access pattern and clocks — treat them as order-of-magnitude anchors, not datasheet
          promises. Tokens/s figures are bandwidth <em>ceilings</em>; real kernels land 70–90%
          of the way there.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

